<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">14 August in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 14 August, from 74 BCE to 1945.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">74 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,099 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Huo Guang has the new Han emperor Liu He deposed</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Officials led by the Western Han minister Huo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> presented articles of impeachment against Liu He to Empress Dowager Shangguan, and the emperor was deposed only 27 days after Huo had installed him. He was later demoted to Marquis of Haihun.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1040">1040 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">986 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">King Duncan I of Scotland is killed by Macbeth</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Duncan I was killed in battle near Elgin by Macbeth, who took the throne. Macbeth ruled for 17 years, a far more capable king than the villain of Shakespeare's play written five centuries later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1264">1264 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">762 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">A Genoese fleet captures a Venetian convoy off Saseno</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-saint-sabas">War of Saint Sabas</a>, a Genoese fleet captured or sank most of the ships of a Venetian trade convoy near Saseno Island, off the coast of Albania. Beaten in every direct clash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> had turned to raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> convoys.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-saint-sabas">War of Saint Sabas</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1385">1385 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">641 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Portugal defeats Castile at the Battle of Aljubarrota</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">John I of Portugal</a> and his constable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-alvares-pereira">Nuno Álvares Pereira</a>, with English allies, decisively defeated the larger army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a>. The victory ended the succession crisis and secured Portugal's independence and the Aviz dynasty.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota">Battle of Aljubarrota</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1383-1385-portuguese-interregnum">1383–1385 Portuguese interregnum</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">John I of Portugal</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1457">1457 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">569 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Mainz Psalter becomes the first dated printed book</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Johann Fust and Peter Schöffer completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>, the first printed book to carry a date and printer's names. It also pioneered colour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> with its red and blue initials.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1900">1900 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">126 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">International forces relieve the besieged legations in Beijing</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Troops of the Eight-Nation Alliance entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and lifted the 55-day siege of the foreign legations by Boxers and Qing troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was forced to pay a crushing indemnity.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1935">1935 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">91 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Franklin Roosevelt signs the Social Security Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Roosevelt signed the Social Security Act, creating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">old-age pensions</a>, unemployment insurance and aid for dependent children. It became the cornerstone of the American welfare state.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1945">1945 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">81 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Japan accepts the Allied terms of surrender</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s government notified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> that it accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a>, and President Truman announced the news that evening. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a> recorded a broadcast to his people for the next day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 14 August <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-iii-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cosimo-iii-de-medici.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Cosimo III de' Medici</span><span class="tp">1642–1723</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Frederick William I of Prussia</span><span class="tp">1688–1740</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-pius-vii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Pius VII</span><span class="tp">1742–1823</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-obrenovic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alexander-obrenovic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alexander Obrenović</span><span class="tp">1876–1903</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/otto-tief"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/otto-tief.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Otto Tief</span><span class="tp">1889–1976</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 14 August <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/duncan-i-of-scotland"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Duncan I of Scotland</span><span class="tp">c. 1001–1040</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-pius-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Pius II</span><span class="tp">1405–1464</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-von-neurath"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/konstantin-von-neurath.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Konstantin von Neurath</span><span class="tp">1873–1956</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mahasi-sayadaw"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mahasi-sayadaw.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mahasi Sayadaw</span><span class="tp">1904–1982</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
